      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3024.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 679,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to truly understand water, we have to look at it through a few different lenses: its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The \"Weird\" Science)\nWater is actually a very strange and unique substance compared to other liquids on Earth. \n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states: **solid** (ice), **liquid** (water), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why water in nature is rarely pure; it carries dissolved minerals, nutrients, and chemicals.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water does the opposite—it expands and becomes *less* dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet. \n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite all that water, only about **2.5% is fresh water** (not salty). Furthermore, over 68% of that fresh water is locked away in glaciers and ice caps, and most of the rest is underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible in lakes and rivers for human use.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop driven by the sun. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the atmosphere, cools and condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into the oceans via rivers.\n\n### 3. The Essence of Life\nBiologically speaking, **water is life.** \n* **Life's Medium:** All known forms of life depend on water. Chemical reactions of life (like metabolism and digestion) take place in a water-based environment inside cells.\n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of about **60% water**. Our brains are roughly 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are about 31% water. We can survive weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the Earth's landscapes, drives our weather and climate, and acts as the fundamental liquid medium that makes biological life possible."
